      },
      "text": "χ (mod c) χ3=χ0 =χ0",
      "html": null,
      "level": null,
      "section_hierarchy": []
    },
    {
      "block_type": "text",
      "box": {
        "page": 0,
        "x0": 0.11764705882352941,
        "y0": 0.6553459595959596,
        "x1": 0.8405866013071894,
        "y1": 0.6704558080808081
      },
      "text": "This is bounded using the large sieve for sextic characters due to Baier and Young [1].",
      "html": null,
      "level": null,
      "section_hierarchy": []
    },
    {
      "block_type": "text",
      "box": {
        "page": 0,
        "x0": 0.11764705882352941,
        "y0": 0.6859520202020202,
        "x1": 0.8823251633986927,
        "y1": 0.7186376262626263
      },
      "text": "1.2.3. Type I2 sums. The obtained Type I and Type II ranges would already be enough for a lower bound of the correct order of magnitude for the number of primes p = x2 + y3. To",
      "html": null,
      "level": null,
      "section_hierarchy": []
    },
    {
      "block_type": "text",
      "box": {
        "page": 0,
        "x0": 0.11764705882352941,
        "y0": 0.7212550505050506,
        "x1": 0.8822973856209151,
        "y1": 0.7539406565656566
      },
      "text": "show an asymptotic formula, we also need to consider the Type I2 sums. We are able to handle these for",
      "html": null,
      "level": null,
      "section_hierarchy": []
    },
    {
      "block_type": "text",
      "box": {
        "page": 0,
        "x0": 0.11764705882352941,
        "y0": 0.7621489898989899,
        "x1": 0.8824199346405229,
        "y1": 0.8609103535353535
      },
      "text": "D2 < X1/4 by using the spectral methods of GL(2) automorphic forms. The proof will appear in an upcoming joint work with Grimmelt [14], as an application of the averages over orbits introduced in [13]. To sketch the idea in the critical case d ∼ D2, m ∼ n ∼ X/D2, note that for a ﬁxed y we consider a variant of the divisor problem along a quadratic polynomial",
      "html": null,
      "level": null,
      "section_hierarchy": []
    },
    {
      "block_type": "picture",
      "box": {
        "page": 0,
        "x0": 0.7839019607843137,
        "y0": 0.8259242424242424,
        "x1": 0.8329215686274509,
        "y1": 0.8265303030303031
      },
      "text": null,
      "html": null,
      "level": null,
      "section_hierarchy": []
    },
    {
      "block_type": "text",
      "box": {
        "page": 0,
        "x0": 0.11764705882352941,
        "y0": 0.861239898989899,
        "x1": 0.8821960784313725,
        "y1": 0.9084305555555555
      },
      "text": "x f(Ax)d(x2 + y3). Applying Poisson summation on x produces the expected main term and an error term with the dual variable of length H ≈",
      "html": null,
      "level": null,
      "section_hierarchy": []
    },
